{"meta": {"chapter": "01", "section": "02", "title": "Free Body Diagrams", "course": "statics", "learning_objectives": ["Draw complete and accurate free body diagrams", "Identify all forces acting on an isolated body", "Apply the systematic 6-step FBD procedure", "Recognize common FBD errors and how to avoid them"], "concept_ids": ["free_body_diagram", "force_identification", "particle_model", "reaction_forces"], "prerequisites": ["equilibrium_condition"], "prereq_section_ids": ["statics/01/01"], "practice_ids": ["fbd_inclined_block", "fbd_pulley_tension", "fbd_contact_force_identify", "fbd_weight_direction"], "estimated_time_minutes": 45}, "content": "# Free Body Diagrams\n\n> **\"The free body diagram is the language through which mechanics problems become solvable.\"**  \n> - R.C. Hibbeler, *Engineering Mechanics: Statics*\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Draw** complete and accurate free body diagrams for particles and simple rigid bodies\n2. **Identify** all external forces acting on an isolated object\n3. **Apply** a reliable six-step FBD workflow before writing equations\n4. **Recognize** common diagram errors that lead to wrong equilibrium equations\n\n---\n\n## Why Free Body Diagrams Matter\n\nIn the previous section, equilibrium was expressed in a compact mathematical form:\n\n$$\n\\sum \\vec{F} = 0\n$$\n\nThat equation looks simple, but it only helps after the correct forces are placed in it. The free body diagram is the step that converts a physical situation into a defensible model.\n\n> **Engineering Reality:** Many statics errors are not algebra errors. They begin much earlier, when an engineer omits a support reaction, draws tension in the wrong direction, or forgets that a surface can only push normal to itself.\n\nThat is why experienced analysts slow down at the diagram stage. A careful free body diagram protects every later step.\n\n> **A Failure That Started on the Diagram:** The 1981 Hyatt Regency walkway collapse in Kansas City killed 114 people. A design change doubled the load that one set of connector rods had to carry, but the connection was never re-drawn as an isolated free body. Had the affected hanger-rod and box-beam been isolated and the actual transferred force placed on the diagram, the doubled demand would have been obvious before fabrication. The arithmetic was not exotic; the missing step was an honest free body diagram of the changed connection.\n\n---\n\n## What a Free Body Diagram Actually Is\n\nA **free body diagram** is a simplified sketch of one isolated object showing:\n\n- the body of interest,\n- all **external** forces acting on it,\n- and a coordinate system for reference.\n\nThe key word is **free**. We imagine cutting the body away from everything touching it. Every removed connection is then replaced by the force or moment that connection exerts.\n\n| Physical Connection | What Appears in the FBD |\n| --- | --- |\n| Cable or rope | Tension along the cable |\n| Smooth surface | Normal force perpendicular to the surface |\n| Rough surface | Normal force plus friction force |\n| Pin support | Two reaction components |\n| Fixed support | Two reaction components plus a moment |\n\nThis replacement step is the heart of modeling. It is where physical intuition enters the mechanics workflow.\n\n---\n\n## The Six-Step FBD Workflow\n\nUse the same sequence every time:\n\n### Step 1: Select the Body\n\nDecide exactly what object you are analyzing. Draw it as simply as possible: a dot, block, bar, or beam.\n\n### Step 2: Isolate It\n\nMentally cut the object away from cables, supports, and surrounding bodies.\n\n### Step 3: Choose Axes\n\nDefine a coordinate system before resolving forces. Standard horizontal and vertical axes work in many problems, but rotated axes are often helpful on inclined surfaces.\n\n### Step 4: Add Body Forces\n\nThe most common body force is weight:\n\n$$\nW = mg\n$$\n\nWeight always acts vertically downward toward Earth's center.\n\n### Step 5: Add Contact and Connection Forces\n\nAt every cut connection, replace the support with the correct force model:\n\n- cable -> tension along the cable\n- smooth contact -> normal reaction only\n- rough contact -> normal and friction\n- pin -> two reaction components\n\n### Step 6: Label Known and Unknown Quantities\n\nMark symbols, angles, and known magnitudes clearly before solving.\n\nDrag the steps into the correct order to internalize the workflow. Notice that isolating the body must come before you can decide which connection forces to add.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Select the single body to analyze\",\"Isolate it by mentally cutting away every cable, support, and contact\",\"Choose convenient axes (tilt them along an incline when helpful)\",\"Add body forces, starting with weight W = mg acting straight down\",\"Replace each cut connection with its force (cable to tension, smooth contact to normal, pin to two components)\",\"Label every known magnitude, angle, and unknown symbol before writing equations\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n> **A Good Habit:** If you cannot explain where each force comes from physically, the diagram is not finished.\n\n---\n\n## Force Identification Checklist\n\nBefore moving on to equilibrium equations, check each category deliberately.\n\n| Category | Question to Ask | Typical Force |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Gravity | Does the object have mass? | Weight $W = mg$ |\n| Cables | Is the object suspended or tied? | Tension $T$ |\n| Contact | Is it touching a surface? | Normal $N$, friction $f$ |\n| Support | Is it pinned, fixed, or guided? | Reaction components |\n| Applied Load | Did the problem state a push or pull? | External force $P$ or $F$ |\n\nThis checklist matters because missing a single external force can invalidate the entire solution.\n\n---\n\n## Choosing Axes: Why a Tilted Frame Pays Off\n\nA free body diagram becomes useful only when forces are resolved into components. On an inclined plane, two choices exist, and they are not equally convenient.\n\nIf we use standard horizontal/vertical axes, the normal force $N$ points along the incline normal, so it splits into two components and *both* equilibrium equations contain *both* unknowns. If instead we rotate the axes so that $x$ runs along the slope and $y$ runs normal to it, then $N$ lies entirely on the $y$ axis and only the weight needs resolving. The two equations then decouple, and each one isolates a single unknown.\n\nFor a plane inclined at angle $\\theta$, a vertical weight $W$ resolves in the tilted frame as:\n\n$$\nW_x = W \\sin\\theta \\quad \\text{(down the slope)}\n$$\n\n$$\nW_y = W \\cos\\theta \\quad \\text{(into the slope)}\n$$\n\nThis small modeling choice removes most of the algebra before it ever appears. We use it directly below.\n\n<inline-check question=\"On a smooth incline at angle theta, why does rotating the axes so x runs along the slope simplify the equilibrium equations?\" explanation=\"With tilted axes the normal force N lies entirely on the y axis, so only the weight needs resolving into W sin(theta) along the slope and W cos(theta) into it. The two equations then each contain a single unknown instead of both.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Because the normal force then lies entirely on one axis, decoupling the two equations.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Because tilting the axes makes the weight disappear from the analysis.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Because friction can be ignored once the axes are tilted.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 1: Block Held on a Smooth Incline\n\n**Problem:** A 20 kg block rests on a smooth incline at $\\theta = 30^\\circ$. A cable parallel to the incline keeps it from sliding. Draw the FBD, then find the cable tension $T$ and the normal reaction $N$.\n\n**Step 1: Isolate and draw the FBD.** Cutting the block free, three external forces appear: the weight $W = mg$ acting vertically down, the normal reaction $N$ perpendicular to the surface, and the cable tension $T$ acting up the slope. Because the surface is smooth, there is no friction.\n\n**Step 2: Choose tilted axes.** Let $x$ point up the slope and $y$ point away from the surface. The weight is the only force that must be resolved:\n\n$$\nW = (20)(9.81) = 196.2 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n$$\nW_x = W \\sin 30^\\circ = 196.2(0.5) = 98.1 \\text{ N (down-slope)}\n$$\n\n$$\nW_y = W \\cos 30^\\circ = 196.2(0.866) = 169.9 \\text{ N (into surface)}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Apply equilibrium along the slope.**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad T - W\\sin 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT = 98.1 \\text{ N} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\boxed{T = 98.1 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Apply equilibrium normal to the slope.**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad N - W\\cos 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nN = 169.9 \\text{ N} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\boxed{N = 169.9 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\n> **Modeling Insight:** Surfaces do not push \"up.\" They push normal to themselves. Notice $N = 169.9$ N is *less* than the weight $196.2$ N, because the slope only has to resist the component of weight pressed into it. A student who draws $N$ straight up gets $N = W$ and an immediately wrong answer.\n\nVary the mass and slope below. Watch how the cable tension (the down-slope demand) and the normal reaction trade off as the incline steepens: at a vertical wall the cable carries the whole weight and N drops to zero.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Block on a smooth incline: T = W sin(theta), N = W cos(theta)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"m\",\"label\":\"Block mass\",\"min\":5,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"kg\",\"default\":20},{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Incline angle\",\"min\":0,\"max\":90,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":30}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Weight W = mg\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m * 9.81\"},{\"label\":\"Cable tension T = W sin(theta)\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m * 9.81 * sin(theta * 3.14159265 / 180)\"},{\"label\":\"Normal reaction N = W cos(theta)\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m * 9.81 * cos(theta * 3.14159265 / 180)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Hanging Mass Over a Frictionless Pulley\n\n**Problem:** A 50 kg mass hangs from a rope that passes over a frictionless pulley and is anchored to a wall. The two rope segments leave the pulley at $90^\\circ$ to each other: one segment vertical (down to the mass), the other horizontal (to the wall anchor). Find the rope tension and the resultant force the rope bundle exerts on the pulley axle.\n\n**Step 1: FBD of the mass.** Two forces act: tension $T$ up and weight $W$ down.\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad T - mg = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT = (50)(9.81) = \\boxed{490.5 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nFor an ideal frictionless pulley with a massless rope, the tension magnitude is identical on both rope segments, so each segment pulls with $490.5$ N.\n\n**Step 2: FBD of the pulley.** Both rope segments pull *away* from the pulley along their own directions. Take $x$ horizontal (toward the wall anchor) and $y$ vertical (down toward the mass). The two equal tensions act along perpendicular directions, so the net rope pull on the pulley is the vector sum:\n\n$$\nR = \\sqrt{T^2 + T^2} = T\\sqrt{2} = 490.5\\,(1.414) = \\boxed{693.7 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\ndirected at $45^\\circ$ between the two rope segments. The axle reaction must equal and oppose this $693.7$ N pull to keep the pulley in equilibrium.\n\nThis example shows why the body of interest must be chosen carefully. The mass and the pulley belong to the same physical system, but they do **not** share a free body diagram: the mass sees one tension and gravity, while the pulley sees two tensions and an axle reaction.\n\n> **Design Note:** A pulley that merely redirects a rope still carries a force larger than the load it serves. Bearings and brackets must be sized for the *resultant* axle force, here $693.7$ N, not for the $490.5$ N hanging weight.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Ring Held by Two Cables and a Horizontal Pull\n\n**Problem:** A small ring is held in equilibrium by two cables and a horizontal applied force $P$. Cable 1 pulls up-and-left at $60^\\circ$ above the horizontal with tension $T_1 = 400$ N. Cable 2 is vertical and supports a 30 kg load, so $T_2 = (30)(9.81) = 294.3$ N acting downward. Find the horizontal force $P$ (acting to the right) and confirm the system is consistent.\n\n**Step 1: FBD of the ring.** Three external forces act at the point: $T_1$ at $60^\\circ$ above horizontal pointing up-left, the vertical downward load $T_2 = 294.3$ N, and the horizontal force $P$ pointing right.\n\n**Step 2: Resolve $T_1$.**\n\n$$\nT_{1x} = T_1\\cos 60^\\circ = 400(0.5) = 200 \\text{ N (to the left)}\n$$\n\n$$\nT_{1y} = T_1\\sin 60^\\circ = 400(0.866) = 346.4 \\text{ N (upward)}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Horizontal equilibrium.** Taking right as positive:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad P - T_{1x} = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nP = 200 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\boxed{P = 200 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Vertical check.** Taking up as positive:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = T_{1y} - T_2 = 346.4 - 294.3 = +52.1 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nThe vertical sum is **not** zero, so with the stated values the ring is *not* in equilibrium; it has a net $52.1$ N upward imbalance. For true equilibrium the vertical cable would need to support $346.4$ N, meaning a load of $346.4 / 9.81 = 35.3$ kg. This is exactly the kind of inconsistency a disciplined FBD exposes before any hardware is built.\n\n> **Takeaway:** Equilibrium is two equations in 2D, not one. A solution that satisfies $\\sum F_x = 0$ can still fail $\\sum F_y = 0$. Always close both.\n\nWork through what a disciplined analyst does when the vertical sum refuses to close.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The ring fails the vertical equilibrium check: what now?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You closed horizontal equilibrium (P = 200 N), but the vertical sum comes out to +52.1 N instead of zero. What is the correct interpretation?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Ignore it; getting one equation to balance is good enough.\",\"next\":\"bad1\",\"feedback\":\"No. 2D equilibrium requires BOTH sums to vanish. A nonzero vertical sum means the body is not in equilibrium as stated.\"},{\"label\":\"The stated loads are inconsistent: the ring is not actually in equilibrium.\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. A nonzero net force is a physical red flag, not a rounding issue.\"}]},\"bad1\":{\"prompt\":\"You shipped the design and the ring drifts upward in service. What did the FBD already warn you about?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"That the vertical forces never balanced, so net 52.1 N pushed it up.\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The diagram told you before any hardware was built.\"}],\"feedback\":\"This is precisely the error a careful FBD is meant to catch.\"},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"To restore equilibrium without changing the cables angle or P, which fix follows directly from the numbers?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Increase the vertical cable load so T2 rises from 294.3 N to 346.4 N (the upward component T1y).\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Matching T2 to T1y = 346.4 N closes the vertical sum exactly.\"},{\"label\":\"Increase P, the horizontal force.\",\"next\":\"bad2\",\"feedback\":\"P only appears in the horizontal equation; changing it cannot fix a vertical imbalance.\"}]},\"bad2\":{\"prompt\":\"Adding horizontal force left the +52.1 N vertical surplus untouched. Where must the correction act?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"On the vertical balance, by raising the supported load to 346.4 N.\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Fix the equation that is actually violated.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Always correct the equation that fails, not a different one.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"What hanging mass corresponds to a corrected load of 346.4 N?\",\"feedback\":\"m = 346.4 / 9.81 = 35.3 kg. Raising the load from 30 kg to 35.3 kg makes T2 = T1y and closes both equilibrium equations. The FBD turned a vague mistake into an exact design correction.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Common Errors\n\n### Missing Weight\n\nIf the body has mass, weight must appear unless the problem explicitly neglects it.\n\n### Wrong Tension Direction\n\nCables and ropes pull away from the object. They do not push toward it.\n\n### Drawing Internal Forces\n\nWhen analyzing one isolated part of a larger system, only draw forces exerted **on that part by something outside it**.\n\n### Replacing Supports Incorrectly\n\nA support model is a mechanical assumption. If a roller is drawn like a pin, the unknown count changes and the entire solution path changes with it.\n\n---\n\n## Design Interpretation\n\nFree body diagrams are not just for textbook equilibrium problems. They are the first filter engineers use when checking whether a proposed design even makes physical sense.\n\nFor example:\n\n- a bracket sketch may look symmetrical, but the FBD can reveal a large hidden moment,\n- a cable layout may appear efficient, but the geometry may create an excessive tension demand,\n- and a sliding mechanism may seem simple until the contact reactions show where friction and wear will concentrate.\n\nThat is why a disciplined FBD habit improves more than homework performance. It improves load-path thinking.\n\n### Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | What the FBD Reveals |\n| --- | --- |\n| Crane and hoist rigging | The resultant axle and sheave forces exceed the lifted load, so bearings must be sized to the vector sum, not the weight |\n| Retaining walls and slopes | Resolving soil weight along tilted axes separates sliding demand from bearing demand |\n| Bolted connections | Isolating one bracket exposes hidden moments and shear that a symmetric sketch hides (the Hyatt Regency lesson) |\n| Cable-stayed and guyed masts | Shallow cable angles drive tension far above the supported load, visible only after components are drawn |\n| Robotic grippers and contacts | Replacing each contact with normal-plus-friction shows where slip and wear concentrate |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same isolate-and-replace discipline scales from a 20 kg block to a 200-tonne bridge segment. The body changes; the workflow does not.\n\n---\n\n## A Practical Self-Check Before Solving\n\nAsk these questions:\n\n- Is the body clearly isolated?\n- Did I include every external force source?\n- Are force directions consistent with the physical connection?\n- Is the coordinate system chosen for convenience?\n- Are there any forces I drew without a physical cause?\n\nIf the answer to the last question is yes, pause and fix the diagram before writing equilibrium equations.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Particle equilibrium (2D) | $\\sum F_x = 0$, $\\sum F_y = 0$ |\n| Weight (body force) | $W = mg$ |\n| Weight on incline (tilted axes) | $W_x = W\\sin\\theta$, $W_y = W\\cos\\theta$ |\n| Resultant of two perpendicular equal tensions | $R = T\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| Cable component resolution | $T_x = T\\cos\\theta$, $T_y = T\\sin\\theta$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- The free body diagram is the modeling foundation of statics; isolate the body before writing any equations.\n- Replace every support or contact with the force it exerts: cable to tension, smooth contact to normal only, rough contact to normal plus friction, pin to two components, fixed support to two components plus a moment.\n- Weight acts vertically downward, tension acts along the cable away from the body, and normal force acts perpendicular to the contact surface, not \"up.\"\n- Rotating axes to align with an inclined surface decouples the equilibrium equations and removes most of the algebra.\n- A pulley or redirect point carries a resultant force larger than the load it serves; size hardware to the vector sum.\n- 2D equilibrium is two equations: satisfying one does not guarantee the other, so always close both $\\sum F_x$ and $\\sum F_y$.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Section 1.3: Two-Force and Three-Force Members**, we will use these diagram skills to recognize special structural cases that can simplify analysis before any heavy algebra begins.\n\n---\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"free_body_diagram\",\"force_identification\",\"particle_model\",\"reaction_forces\"]'></concept-map>\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Free Body Diagram Review\" context=\"A manufacturing engineer is troubleshooting a jammed pulley assembly and needs to isolate the carriage, cable, and guide-contact forces correctly before calculating whether the rail is overloaded.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"What is the primary purpose of a free body diagram in statics?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"To isolate one body and show all external forces acting on it.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"To list every microscopic force inside the material.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"To avoid defining axes and sign conventions before solving.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. A free body diagram isolates the body of interest and replaces surrounding connections with the external forces they exert.\"></interactive-quiz>\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "fbd_inclined_block", "type": "numeric", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["free_body_diagram", "force_components", "reaction_forces"], "stem": "A 30 kg block rests on a smooth inclined plane at 40 degrees to the horizontal. Calculate the normal force exerted by the surface on the block.", "final_answer": {"value": 225.4, "unit": "N", "tolerance": 0.5}, "hint": "The normal force balances the component of weight perpendicular to the surface."}, {"id": "fbd_pulley_tension", "type": "numeric", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["free_body_diagram", "reaction_forces"], "stem": "A 25 kg mass hangs from a rope that passes over a frictionless pulley. What is the tension in the rope?", "final_answer": {"value": 245.25, "unit": "N", "tolerance": 0.5}, "hint": "For the hanging mass at rest, the only vertical forces are tension and weight."}, {"id": "fbd_contact_force_identify", "type": "multiple_choice", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["free_body_diagram", "force_identification", "reaction_forces"], "stem": "A block rests on a smooth horizontal table. Which contact force must appear on its free body diagram?", "options": ["Only friction", "Only a normal force perpendicular to the surface", "A cable tension and a moment", "No contact force at all"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A smooth contact surface provides a normal reaction perpendicular to the surface."}, {"id": "fbd_weight_direction", "type": "multiple_choice", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["free_body_diagram", "force_identification"], "stem": "Which statement about weight on a free body diagram is correct?", "options": ["Weight acts perpendicular to the supporting surface", "Weight acts along the nearest cable", "Weight acts vertically downward toward Earth's center", "Weight can be omitted if the object is at rest"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Weight always acts vertically downward, regardless of nearby surface orientation.", "misconception_id": "forget_weight_on_fbd"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "forget_weight_on_fbd", "pattern": "concept_mixup", "description": "Leaving weight off the free body diagram", "trigger": "learner draws support or tension forces but omits gravity on a massive object", "feedback": "Any object with mass has weight. If the body is isolated, weight must appear on the free body diagram unless the problem explicitly neglects it.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "tension_points_toward_object", "pattern": "direction_error", "description": "Drawing tension toward the object instead of away along the cable", "trigger": "learner points cable tension into the body", "feedback": "An ideal cable can only pull, not push. Tension on the isolated body acts along the cable and away from the body.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "internal_forces_on_fbd", "pattern": "force_identification", "description": "Adding internal forces that should not appear on the isolated body", "trigger": "learner includes forces from parts that are inside the chosen system boundary", "feedback": "A free body diagram should show only external forces acting on the isolated body or subsystem.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}